## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of low-latency IPC, NUMA locality, and cache-coherence with careful memory ordering. It also probes design trade-offs for per-core buffering vs a centralized log.

## Key Concepts
- Per-core buffering and cross-core publication
- Sequence-numbered slots and wrap-around handling
- Memory ordering on x86-64 (relaxed/upholds with fences)
- False sharing avoidance and cacheline padding
- Validation: throughput, ordering, and backpressure under contention

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-enqueue
bool enqueue(int core_id, void* msg) {
  uint64_t pos = atomic_fetch_add(&tail[core_id], 1);
  slot_t* s = &ring[core_id][pos & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != pos) return false; // consumer hasn't advanced
  s->payload = msg;
  s->seq = pos + 1;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to support dynamic growth of the log while preserving wait-freedom?
- How would you test correctness with synthetic workloads that stress cross-core contention?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of crash‑consistency in non‑volatile memory, proper cache‑line flush semantics, and cross‑socket ordering. It probes how to design durable queues and recoveries with minimal latency impact.

## Key Concepts
- PMEM durability and flush/fence ordering (clwb/clflushopt, sfence)
- Release/acquire memory ordering and per‑slot sequencing
- Crash recovery and replay correctness in a bounded buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo enqueue (high level, non‑blocking semantics)
function enqueue(slot, payload){
  slot.payload = payload; // write payload
  slot.len = payload.length;
  slot.seq = slot.seq + 1; // release semantics
  clwb(&slot.payload); clwb(&slot.len); clwb(&slot.seq);
  sfence();
}
```

```javascript
// Pseudo dequeue
function dequeue(){
  if (ring.head.seq == ring.tail.seq) return null; // empty
  let s = ring.head; let payload = s.payload;
  // advance head after acquire read
  ring.head = next(s);
  s.done = true; clwb(&s.done); sfence();
  return payload;
}
```

