- How would you adapt for multiple producers? 
- How would you test with bursty traffic and verify timing guarantees?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of memory layout, overlap handling, and cache-friendly copy strategies in tight, real-world paths.

## Key Concepts
- Overlap-safe memmove
- Cache-friendly 64-byte blocks
- Alignment assumptions and tail handling
- Fallback to memcpy for tiny or unaligned data
- Microbenchmark plan to measure throughput vs standard memcpy

## Code Example
```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

void *memmove64(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n){
  unsigned char *d = (unsigned char*)dst;
  const unsigned char *s = (const unsigned char*)src;
  const size_t BLOCK = 64;

  if ((size_t)(d) <= (size_t)(s) || d >= s + n){
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      d += BLOCK; s += BLOCK; n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d, s, n);
  } else {
    d += n; s += n;
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      d -= BLOCK; s -= BLOCK;
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d - n, s - n, n);
  }
  return dst;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for arbitrary alignment and larger data sizes?
- Compare performance to builtin memmove on x86-64 with different runtimes.
- What edge cases or architectures could break this approach?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of cache-like structures, address translation, and replacement policies at low level. 

## Key Concepts
- TLB structure and page number tagging
- Linear probing and wraparound
- LRU replacement and correctness in a small cache
- Interaction with two-level page tables during misses

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint32_t tag; uint32_t pa; uint8_t valid; uint8_t lru; } tlb_entry;
static tlb_entry tlb[256];
void tlb_init() { for (int i=0;i<256;i++) tlb[i].valid=0; }
bool tlb_lookup(uint32_t va, uint32_t* pa){ uint32_t page = va >> 12; uint32_t idx = page & 0xFF; for(int i=0;i<256;i++){ tlb_entry* e = &tlb[(idx+i)&0xFF]; if(!e->valid) continue; if(e->tag==page){ *pa = e->pa; e->lru = 0; return true; } e->lru++; } return false; }
void tlb_insert(uint32_t va, uint32_t pa){ uint32_t page = va >> 12; uint32_t idx = page & 0xFF; int miss_pos = -1; uint8_t oldest = 0; for(int i=0;i<256;i++){ tlb_entry* e = &tlb[(idx+i)&0xFF]; if(!e->valid){ miss_pos = (idx+i)&0xFF; break; } if(e->lru > oldest){ oldest = e->lru; miss_pos = (idx+i)&0xFF; } }
 tlb[miss_pos].tag = page; tlb[miss_pos].pa = pa; tlb[miss_pos].valid = 1; tlb[miss_pos].lru = 0; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to multi-core without races?
- What trade-offs arise if you instead make it a set-associative cache?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of atomic operations, memory ordering, and cache behavior. A ticket lock is simple yet reveals reasoning about spin-waiting and fairness.

## Key Concepts

- Atomic operations and memory_order semantics
- FIFO fairness and starvation risk
- Cache-line padding to avoid false sharing
- Busy-wait vs back-off strategies

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
typedef struct {
  _Atomic_uint next;
  _Atomic_uint serve;
} ticket_lock_t;

void lock(ticket_lock_t *t){
  unsigned my = atomic_fetch_add(&t->next, 1);
  while(atomic_load_explicit(&t->serve, memory_order_acquire) != my) {
    // optional pause
  }
}

void unlock(ticket_lock_t *t){
  atomic_fetch_add(&t->serve, 1);
}
```

